  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "A string compared as a text is a build error (LOC017): text.Equals(\"Orders\"), list.Contains(name), Array.IndexOf(texts, name), and counts[name] on a dictionary of texts. They compare texts, not the wording a text shows, and a string becomes a text without translations, which equals no translated text, so the comparison was false while the text showed that wording. Compare the wording with ==, as in text == \"Orders\", or cast the string to LocalizableText to compare texts. A comparer you pass along, as in list.Contains(name, comparer), may compare the wording, so that call passes. The fix in an IDE turns text.Equals(wording) into text == wording.\n\nA LanguageCode that is no culture name, such as \"en_GB\", is build error LOC018: the set would throw as it registers.\n\nThe generated class holds nothing but the texts: the constant ResourceKeyPrefix is gone, so a text can be named ResourceKeyPrefix, and LOC014 is left with a text named like the class itself. Read the prefix from a text's key, as in ShopTranslations.Checkout.Key, which is \"Shop.Checkout\".\n\nA set whose language the build cannot read, because its LanguageCode is no constant, is no longer reported as a missing language, and the placeholders of a text a set inherits from a base class are held to those of the contract as well (LOC016).\n\nHow the package works is in docs/how-it-works.md, which the README links to.\n\nVersion 0.6.0 was not published, so its changes come with this version:\n\nA text written inline is LocalizableText.Inline(en: \"Checkout\", nl: \"Afrekenen\"), where it was LocalizableText.Translated. That is the name the docs and the messages of the build already gave it: an inline text.\n\nA set implements ITranslationSet, where it was ITranslations: public partial class ShopTranslationsEN : ITranslationSet. The interface names one set, as the docs do. The generated contract keeps its name, such as IShopTranslations, and so do the generated class and TranslationsRegistry.\n\nThree parameters are named after the set: Register and RegisterDefaults take sets, ResourceText.GetTranslation takes set, and ITranslationSet.GetLanguageCode takes setType. Only a call that names such an argument needs the new name.\n\nTo move a project, replace LocalizableText.Translated with LocalizableText.Inline, and ITranslations with ITranslationSet. The messages of the build and the fixes in an IDE use the new names.",
